{
  peppy_schema: "contract/v1",
  manifest: {
    name: "sensor_mounts",
    tag: "v1",
    description: [
      "Where a robot carries its sensors: the pose of each of its sensors, such as a camera, a lidar or a range sensor, in the robot frame, now.",
      "camera_geometry gives a camera's own geometry and says that where the camera sits on the robot is not the camera's to know; this contract is where the robot says it. lidar_2d, lidar_3d and range_sensor say the same of where their sensor sits. A consumer that turns a pixel and a depth into a point of the camera's frame through camera_geometry carries that point into the robot frame through the pose reported here.",
      "The values are the robot's design values, and on a walking base its measured tilt and height: exact in a simulation, nominal on hardware, where a sensor stands within the tolerance of its mount. They are not a calibration, and nothing here says how far a physical sensor stands from its design pose.",
      "Conventions:",
      "Robot frame. Fixed to the robot's base: its origin is the point the base stands on, +X points the way the robot faces, +Y to its left and +Z up. It moves with the robot, and it is not a frame of the room or of a simulated world. On a robot with a mobile base, it is the robot frame of base_state.",
      "Sensor pose. The pose of the frame that the sensor's contract defines, which sensor_kinds names. For a camera it is the colour optical frame as camera_geometry defines it: +X to the right of the image, +Y down it, +Z along the viewing direction. For a lidar or a range sensor it is the sensor frame as lidar_2d, lidar_3d or range_sensor defines it: +X forward or along the cone, +Y left, +Z up. A point p of that frame is R(q) * p + t of the robot frame, with t the sensor's position and q its orientation.",
      "Now. A sensor's pose is computed from what the robot measured at `timestamp`: the joints of the part that carries it, such as an arm, a head or a torso, and on a walking base the tilt of the base link and its height above the floor. A sensor fixed to a wheeled base has one pose for the life of the robot.",
    ],
  },
  interfaces: {
    services: [
      {
        name: "get_sensor_poses",
        description: "Give the pose of every sensor the robot's design carries, such as a camera, a lidar or a range sensor, in the robot frame, at `timestamp`. A sensor of the design that no node streams is listed too, under the name its node runs under once one streams it. A robot without sensors answers with empty arrays. A robot that has not measured its joints yet gives an error reply with `precondition_failed`.",
        response_message_format: {
          timestamp: { $type: "time", $description: "When the joints, and on a walking base the base link's tilt and height, that the poses are computed from were measured." },
          sensor_names: { $type: "array", $items: "string", $description: "The names the robot's sensors run under (chest, wrist_left, lidar), in the robot's fixed order. sensor_kinds, positions, orientations and carried_by are index-aligned with this array." },
          sensor_kinds: { $type: "array", $items: "string", $description: "The contract each sensor streams on, which names the frame its pose places, such as \"rgb_camera\", \"rgbd_camera\", \"lidar_2d\", \"lidar_3d\" or \"range_sensor\", in sensor_names order." },
          positions: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $description: "3 entries per sensor (x, y, z, m), in sensor_names order." },
          orientations: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $description: "4 entries per sensor, a unit quaternion [x, y, z, w], in sensor_names order." },
          carried_by: { $type: "array", $items: "string", $description: "The part that carries each sensor, by the name its contract addresses it under, such as an arm's name, or \"\" for a sensor fixed to the base." },
        },
      },
    ],
  },
}
